    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d239">

                <pb facs="0529" n="495" xml:id="pg495"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">750G.00/8–1354</note>
                <head>No. 239<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Robert G Hooker of the Bureau of European
                        Affairs to the Chief United States Negotiator in London
                    (Thompson)</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret<lb/>personal
                            informal</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1954-08-13">August 13, 1954</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Tommy</hi>: I enclose for your information a
                    copy of a rather turgid memorandum I have just written. I have not yet discussed
                    it with any one and have no idea to what extent, if at all, any one agrees with
                    it. It was written before receipt of Belgrade’s 122<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d239-f1"/> and before any report from you on the instructions
                    Velebit was to have received yesterday.</p>
                <p>I have at long last gotten my security clearance and hope it will mean getting
                    into the field soon. At this late stage in the Trieste negotiations I refrain
                    from making the suggestion I might have made earlier, that Leonard<anchor n="2"
                        xml:id="d239-f2"/> and I switch places. I have a feeling that at this point
                    the continuity should be maintained and that he will be able to serve you better
                    than I could.</p>
                <p>You have been having a hell of a time, and I am sorry for you.</p>
                <p>All the best.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d239-f3"/></p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bob</hi></signed></closer>


                <xi:include href="d239-a1.xml" xpointer="d239-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d239-f1" n="1" xml:id="d239-f1-content">
                <p>See <ref target="#d238-f1">footnote 1, <hi rendition="#italic"
                    >supra</hi></ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d239-f2" n="2" xml:id="d239-f2-content">
                <p>Leonard Unger.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d239-f3" n="3" xml:id="d239-f3-content">
                <p>Hooker added the following handwritten postscript: “Bedell and the
                    Secretary—especially Bedell—have been for cracking down on the Italians. My memo
                    is intended to help Livie [Livingston Merchant] and Jamie [James Bonbright]
                    (Wally [Walworth Barbour] is on vacation through August) keep them in line.”</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
